area.\nL46: \nL47: cite39†Join EFF Lists†eff.org L48: \nL49:   * cite40†Copyright (CC BY) L50:   * cite41†Trademark L51:   * cite42†Privacy Policy L52:   * cite43†Thanks L53: \nL54: cite44†Electronic Frontier Foundation L55: \nL56: cite45†Donate†supporters.eff.org L57: \nL58: cite44†Electronic Frontier Foundation L59:   * cite1†About L60:     * cite2†Contact L61:     * cite3†Press L62:     * cite4†People L63:     * cite5†Opportunities L64:   * cite6†Issues L65:     * cite7†Free Speech L66:     * cite8†Privacy L67:     * cite9†Creativity and Innovation L68:     * cite10†Transparency L69:     * cite11†International L70:     * cite12†Security L71:     * cite13†Artificial Intelligence L72:   * cite14†Our Work L73:     * cite15†Deeplinks Blog L74:     * cite16†Press Releases L75:     * cite17†Events L76:     * cite18†Legal Cases L77:     * cite19†Whitepapers L78:     * cite20†Podcasts L79:     * cite21†Annual Reports L80:   * cite22†Take Action†act.eff.org L81:     * cite22†Action Center†act.eff.org L82:     * cite23†Volunteer L83:     * cite24†Follow EFF L84:   * cite25†Tools L85:     * cite26†Privacy Badger L86:     * cite27†Surveillance Self-Defense L87:     * cite28†Certbot L88:     * cite29†Atlas of Surveillance L89:     * cite30†Cover Your Tracks L90:     * cite31†Street Level Surveillance†sls.eff.org L91:     * cite32†apkeep L92:   * cite33†Shop†shop.eff.org L93:   * cite34†Donate†supporters.eff.org L94:     * cite34†Donate to EFF†supporters.eff.org L95:     * cite33†Shop†shop.eff.org L96:     * cite35†Giving Societies L97:     * cite36†Sponsorships L98:     * cite37†Other Ways to Give L99:     * cite38†Membership FAQ L100: # EFF to Lawmakers: Ground AI Cybersecurity Rules in Best Practices\nL101: \nL102: With doomsday AI scenarios dominating the news, lawmakers are rightly concerned about reports concerning security breaches at major US AI labs, such as the OpenAI–Hugging Face incident and the many others reported in its aftermath. As they consider potentially regulating frontier AI, they should focus any new legislation on the cite46†immediate, demonstrated risks from those incidents.\nL103: cite47†Post-incident†cdn.openai.com cite48†reports†metr.org show that the Hugging Face incident could have been mitigated or prevented by following longstanding cybersecurity best practices, like stronger sandboxing and monitoring. Any new legislation should focus on closing gaps in existing law to prevent AI companies from taking unreasonable risks with the public's security.\nL104: When an AI developer or deployer runs a test or a task that has a high likelihood of causing harm to third parties—for instance, by breaking into someone else's computers—there should be clear minimum safety requirements. Such tests should run in a properly sandboxed test environment, disconnected from other systems, and be monitored and logged. Following these fundamental best practices would have prevented or substantially mitigated all of the incidents at AI labs that we currently know about.\nL105: That said, any proposal must be flexible enough to evolve with changing technology. Minimum safety requirements specific only to current AI technologies are likely to become obsolete; legal standards tied to well-established cybersecurity best practices are far more likely to stand the test of time. Tying any new mandates to evidence-backed security protocols also protects the public without impeding future AI development.\nL106: Strong legislation should also mandate and fund independent third-party investigations into any serious security incidents that may occur during AI labs’ tests of new tools, and make reports of these investigations available to the public. This important transparency measure would go a long way toward providing public oversight of the industry.\nL107: \nL108: As with any technology regulation, those targeting cybersecurity practices at AI labs must be cite49†careful, precise, and practical .\nL109: ## Related Issues\nL110: \nL111: cite13†Artificial Intelligence L112: \nL113: cite12†Security L114: \nL115: ## Related Updates\nL116: \nL117: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite51†Rindala Alajaji , cite52†Tori Noble , cite53†Jacob Hoffman-Andrews , cite54†Hayley Tsukayama | September 18, 2026\nL118: ### cite55†EFF Statement on California Governor's Executive Order on AI L119: \nL120: It's an opportunity for a needed, thoughtful conversation about artificial intelligence and its potential harms. Everyday Californians are feeling real anxiety about the risks of artificial intelligence, and as an organization that works to ensure technology empowers people, EFF welcomes this order as a way for California to lead a...\nL121: \nL122: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite56†Erica Portnoy , cite57†Thorin Klosowski | September 18, 2026\nL123: ### cite58†Secure Messaging and AI Remain In Conflict Despite the Promise of TEEs L124: \nL125: Secure messaging platforms, like Signal, WhatsApp, and recently, cite59†encrypted RCS , operate on a straightforward assumption: the content at each end of a conversation is private to the participants in the conversation. End-to-end encryption helps provide the mathematical guarantees that the companies who operate these messaging platforms cannot access the...\nL126: \nL127: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite60†Lena Cohen | September 8, 2026\nL128: ### cite61†New Records Reveal Problems with Medicare’s AI Prior Authorization Experiment L129: \nL130: EFF sued the government for information a new Medicare program that uses AI to evaluate prior authorization requests for certain medical services. Today, we’re releasing approximately 1,000 pages of records obtained through this litigation. The records show that the program has resulted in widespread delays and denials of care, operational...\nL131: \nL132: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite62†Matthew Guariglia | September 1, 2026\nL133: ### cite63†Judge Rules DOD Unlawfully Retaliated Against Anthropic L134: \nL135: A federal judge has sided with Anthropic on its claims that the Department of Defense illegally retaliated against Anthropic’s protected speech by labeling the AI company a “supply chain risk.”\nL136: \nL137: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite52†Tori Noble , cite64†Corynne McSherry | August 31, 2026\nL138: ### cite65†EFF to Courts: Don’t Rewrite Copyright Over AI Hype L139: \nL140: New markets, new ideas, and new creators are actually what copyright is supposed to promote, not restrict. Using copyright to lock in existing gatekeepers and massive rightsholders’ profits helps neither the public nor individual artists.\nL141: \nL142: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite66†Hugh D'Andrade | August 11, 2026\nL143: ### cite67†Who (or What) Generates Images for EFF? L144: \nL145: We’ve had a few questions from EFF supporters lately, asking whether the images we use on our blog posts, or on cite68†donation†supporters.eff.org and cite69†shop†shopeff.org items, have been created with AI image generators. We’d like to answer these questions and clarify our internal policy...\nL146: \nL147: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite51†Rindala Alajaji , cite52†Tori Noble | August 3, 2026\nL148: ### cite70†EFF Joins 18 Civil Rights Organizations Calling on Governor Hochul to Reject the Stealth Crawler Prohibition Act L149: \nL150: EFF joined a group of 18 civil society organizations to send a letter encouraging New York Governor Kathy Hochul to veto cite71†Senate Bill 9934A, the New York Stealth Crawler Prohibition Act†www.nysenate.gov . The letter states:While framed as a measure to protect local journalism, this legislation harms free expression and establishes...\nL151: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite52†Tori Noble , cite54†Hayley Tsukayama | August 3, 2026\nL152: ### cite72†EFF Joins Call for FTC to Drop Its Disastrous AI Policy Proposal L153: \nL154: The Federal Trade Commission issued a proposed policy statement \"concerning the suppression of accuracy in artificial intelligence systems.\" But the government may not install itself as the arbiter of truth. We urge the FTC to withdraw this misguided proposal and instead focus on its core strengths and mission to protect...\nL155: \nL156: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite52†Tori Noble | July 20, 2026\nL157: ### cite73†“Stealth Crawlers” Are Not a Threat to the Open Web. Bills Targeting Them Would Be. L158: \nL159: There’s a new boogeyman in the battles over AI: so-called “stealth crawlers.” We’ll admit it—the term “stealth crawlers” sounds quite nefarious. In reality, they’re anything but.“Stealth crawlers” are simply automated tools to access and collect public web data—without disclosing the user’s identity. Private crawlers like these facilitate all kinds of...\nL160: cite50†Deeplinks Blog by cite74†Jillian C. York , cite64†Corynne McSherry | July 10, 2026\nL161: ### cite75†Automated Moderation Is Here to Stay—Accountability Must Keep Pace L162: \nL163: This post is part 2 in a series about automated content moderation. Read the first post cite76†here .When whistleblower Frances Haugen leaked a set of documents from Meta in 2020, among the revelations was a jarring statistic: The company’s algorithms designed to detect terrorist content incorrectly cite77†deleted nonviolent Arabic-language†www.politico.eu ...\nL164: ## Related Issues\nL165: \nL166: cite13†Artificial Intelligence L167: \nL168: cite12†Security L169: \nL170: cite0†Back to top L171: \nL172: ## Follow EFF:\nL173: \nL174:   * cite78†mastodon†mastodon.social L175:   * cite79†facebook†www.facebook.com L176:   * cite80†instagram†www.instagram.com L177:   * cite81†Blue Sky†bsky.app L178:   * cite82†youtube†www.youtube.com L179:   * cite83†flicker†www.flickr.com L180:   * cit